The == operator compares the references of the objects, not their values. This means that if two different objects contain the same value, they will not be considered equal, even if they seem identical.

On the other hand, the .equals() method compares the actual values of the objects. If two objects contain the same value, they will be considered equal, regardless of their reference.

Here's an example:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

// Using == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal");
} // Output: The strings are not equal

// Using .equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal");
} // Output: The strings are equal
```

In general, you should use the .equals() method to compare the values of objects, especially when dealing with user-input strings or any other cases where the actual content is important. The == operator is better for comparing references, such as when you need to check if two variables point to the same object.

However, it's important to note that .equals() can throw a `NullPointerException` if the object it's being called on is `null`. To avoid this, you can use the `==` operator to check if the object is `null` before calling `.equals()`:

```java
if (str1 == null && str2 == null) {
    System.out.println("Both strings are null");
} else if (str1 == null || str2 == null) {
    System.out.println("At least one string is null");
} else {
    if (str1.equals(str2)) {
        System.out.println("The strings are equal");
    } else {
        System.out.println("The strings are not equal");
    }
}
```

This way, you ensure that you're not trying to call `.equals()` on `null`, which would result in a `NullPointerException`.

